\title{Strange Attractors as Algebraic Objects:\\Finite de Bruijn Approximants and a Binary Prefix Inverse Limit}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 28, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a finite directed-graph model for symbolic trajectories and prove that its inverse limit retains every infinite binary itinerary without identification. At level $n$, vertices are binary words of length $n$, so the level has exactly $2^n$ vertices. Dynamics is represented by the binary de Bruijn edge relation: a word points to another when its suffix agrees with the other's prefix. Deleting the final symbol defines a bonding map between consecutive levels, and this map preserves directed edges. The inverse limit consists of families of words compatible under all truncations. Sending an infinite binary stream to its family of finite prefixes is injective; consequently, the inverse limit contains a full binary Cantor family and is infinite. We provide proofs, algorithms, examples, and applications to symbolic dynamics, while distinguishing this prototype from any Lorenz-specific identification. The construction supplies a finite combinatorial substrate for later topological, cohomological, and dynamical comparisons with strange attractors.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Strange attractors unite deterministic evolution with apparent unpredictability. A smooth map or differential equation may have a short specification, while its long-term orbits create folded, recurrent structures that resist direct classification. Numerical plots reveal geometry, but structural analysis asks which finite observations are compatible, how different resolutions relate, and whether all finite records recover an infinite trajectory.

Symbolic dynamics replaces phase-space points by itineraries through a finite partition. With two regions labeled $0$ and $1$, an orbit is encoded by a stream in $\bits^{\N}$. A finite observer sees only a bounded word. The natural combinatorial object for such observations is a de Bruijn graph, whose vertices are finite words and whose directed edges represent one-step shifts of the observation window.

We construct a tower of binary de Bruijn graphs linked by truncation. Its inverse limit is the set of coherent finite observations at all depths. Our main result says that every infinite binary stream determines a different point of this limit. Thus infinite symbolic information is faithfully retained by a system assembled from finite levels.

This is a foundational model, not an identification of the Lorenz, H\'enon, or R\"ossler attractor. A theorem of that kind requires a specified dynamical system, a justified symbolic partition, and a comparison of topology and dynamics. The present results establish the abstract finite-prefix mechanism needed for such a program.

\section{Binary words and de Bruijn graphs}

Let $\B=\bits$. For $n\in\N$, define the set of words of length $n$ by
\[
W_n=\B^{\{0,1,\ldots,n-1\}}.
\]
Write $w\in W_n$ as $w=(w_0,\ldots,w_{n-1})$. The sole element of $W_0$ is the empty word.

\begin{theorem}[Finite-level cardinality]
For every $n\in\N$,
\[
|W_n|=2^n.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Each of the $n$ coordinates is independently chosen from a two-element set. The multiplication principle gives $2^n$ words. For $n=0$, both sides equal $1$.
\end{proof}

For $n\in\N$, let $G_n$ be the directed graph with vertex set $W_{n+1}$. For $u,v\in W_{n+1}$, define $u\to v$ precisely when
\[
u_{i+1}=v_i\qquad(0\leq i<n).
\]
Thus the suffix of $u$ of length $n$ equals the prefix of $v$ of length $n$. This is the binary de Bruijn graph of order $n+1$.

\begin{example}
In $G_2$, there is an edge $010\to101$ because the suffix $10$ equals the prefix $10$. There is no edge $010\to111$.
\end{example}

\begin{lemma}[Successor description]
Every vertex $u=(u_0,\ldots,u_n)$ in $G_n$ has exactly the two outgoing neighbors
\[
(u_1,\ldots,u_n,0),\qquad (u_1,\ldots,u_n,1),
\]
and exactly the two incoming neighbors
\[
(0,u_0,\ldots,u_{n-1}),\qquad(1,u_0,\ldots,u_{n-1}).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The overlap equations determine every coordinate of a successor except its final coordinate, which has two choices. The incoming statement follows by the same argument at the first coordinate.
\end{proof}

\section{Truncation and edge preservation}

Define
\[
\tau_n:W_{n+1}\longrightarrow W_n,
\qquad
\tau_n(w_0,\ldots,w_n)=(w_0,\ldots,w_{n-1}).
\]
This map deletes the final symbol and is surjective, since each word has the extensions obtained by appending $0$ or $1$.

\begin{theorem}[Edge preservation under truncation]
Let $u,v\in W_{n+2}$. If $u\to v$ in $G_{n+1}$, then
\[
\tau_{n+1}(u)\to\tau_{n+1}(v)
\]
in $G_n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $u=(u_0,\ldots,u_{n+1})$ and $v=(v_0,\ldots,v_{n+1})$. The hypothesis gives $u_{i+1}=v_i$ for every $0\leq i<n+1$. An edge between the truncated words requires these equalities only for $0\leq i<n$. They are a subset of the assumed equalities.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Iterated edge preservation]
Any finite sequence of truncations carries a fine-level edge to an edge at each lower level where the truncated windows are defined.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Apply the preceding theorem repeatedly.
\end{proof}

Hence coarse observation is dynamically consistent: a transition permitted at fine resolution remains permitted after final symbols are forgotten.

\section{The prefix inverse limit}

\begin{definition}[Compatible thread]
A compatible prefix thread is a sequence $x=(x_0,x_1,\ldots)$ such that $x_n\in W_n$ and
\[
\tau_n(x_{n+1})=x_n
\qquad\text{for every }n\in\N.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Prefix inverse limit]
The prefix inverse limit is
\[
L=\varprojlim(W_n,\tau_n)
 =\left\{(x_n)_{n\geq0}:x_n\in W_n,
 \ \tau_n(x_{n+1})=x_n\text{ for all }n\right\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Nested-prefix property]
If $x\in L$ and $m\leq n$, then $x_m$ is the length-$m$ prefix of $x_n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Iterate the compatibility equation $n-m$ times, deleting one final symbol at each step.
\end{proof}

Let $\Sigma_2=\B^{\N}$ be the set of infinite binary streams. For $s=(s_0,s_1,\ldots)$, define
\[
p_n(s)=(s_0,\ldots,s_{n-1})\in W_n
\]
and
\[
\Phi:\Sigma_2\longrightarrow L,
\qquad \Phi(s)=(p_n(s))_{n\geq0}.
\]

\begin{lemma}[Prefix compatibility]
For every $s\in\Sigma_2$, the family $\Phi(s)$ lies in $L$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Deleting the final symbol $s_n$ from $(s_0,\ldots,s_n)$ gives $(s_0,\ldots,s_{n-1})$. Hence $\tau_n(p_{n+1}(s))=p_n(s)$ for every $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Injective stream representation]
The map $\Phi:\Sigma_2\to L$ is injective. Thus distinct infinite binary streams determine distinct compatible threads.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $\Phi(s)=\Phi(t)$. Fix $k\in\N$. Equality at level $k+1$ gives
\[
p_{k+1}(s)=p_{k+1}(t).
\]
Their coordinates at index $k$ are therefore equal, so $s_k=t_k$. Since $k$ was arbitrary, $s=t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Infinitude and Cantor family]
The inverse limit $L$ is infinite and contains an injective image of the entire binary stream set $\Sigma_2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The streams having a single $1$ in position $k$ and zeros elsewhere are pairwise distinct. Their images are pairwise distinct by injectivity. The stronger claim is exactly the existence of the injective map $\Phi$.
\end{proof}

The term ``Cantor family'' is set-theoretic here. With the product topology, $\Sigma_2$ is Cantor space, but no topology on $L$ is required for the results above. A natural future construction reads a stream from $x\in L$ by setting
\[
\Psi(x)_k=(x_{k+1})_k.
\]
Compatibility suggests that $\Psi$ is inverse to $\Phi$; proving this and then a homeomorphism is a further step.

\section{Algorithms and complexity}

To enumerate $W_n$, list the integers from $0$ to $2^n-1$ in binary with exactly $n$ digits. The output itself occupies $\Theta(n2^n)$ bits. To generate the edges of $G_n$, shift each word left and append $0$ and $1$. This produces two outgoing edges per vertex in $O(n2^{n+1})$ time with explicit word copying, or linear word-operation time with packed representations.

An edge test for $u,v\in W_{n+1}$ compares $u_{i+1}$ with $v_i$ for $0\leq i<n$, requiring $O(n)$ time and constant auxiliary space. A finite family $(x_0,\ldots,x_N)$ is checked by verifying $|x_n|=n$ and $x_{n+1}[0:n]=x_n$ for each $n<N$. Explicit comparisons cost $O(N^2)$ total time; persistent prefix storage reduces this to $O(N)$ link checks.

For two stream samples, scan until the first disagreement $k$. Their threads agree through level $k$ and first differ at level $k+1$. This requires $O(k+1)$ time and constant extra space. Failure to find a disagreement in finite samples proves only equality of the observed prefixes.

\section{Examples}

\begin{example}[Periodic trajectory]
For $s=010101\cdots$, the prefixes begin
\[
(),\ (0),\ (0,1),\ (0,1,0),\ (0,1,0,1),\ldots.
\]
Sliding a window of length $3$ alternates along the directed cycle
\[
010\longrightarrow101\longrightarrow010
\]
in $G_2$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Delayed separation]
Let $s=001011\cdots$ and $t=001111\cdots$. They first differ at index $3$. Their threads agree through level $3$, but level $4$ contains respectively
\[
(0,0,1,0)\neq(0,0,1,1).
\]
This is the finite witness promised by injectivity.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Growth]
At levels $0$ through $10$, the vertex counts are
\[
1,2,4,8,16,32,64,128,256,512,1024.
\]
Every level is finite, but each added bit doubles the number of states.
\end{example}

\section{Applications and interpretation}

In symbolic dynamics, the left shift $\sigma(s)_i=s_{i+1}$ advances time. A de Bruijn edge records this sliding-window motion, whereas truncation removes the final symbol and lowers resolution. The edge-preservation theorem links these axes: admissible temporal movement survives coarsening. Once the reverse stream--thread correspondence is established, one can define a shift $\widehat\sigma$ on threads and seek the conjugacy
\[
\Phi\circ\sigma=\widehat\sigma\circ\Phi.
\]

For subshifts of finite type, not every word is admissible. A finite transition matrix forbids selected patterns. The same architecture applies after restricting $W_n$ to admissible words, provided truncation preserves admissibility. This constrained setting is more appropriate for attractors arising from Markov partitions.

The overlap rule also appears in sequence assembly. Finite blocks are joined when suffixes match prefixes. An ideal compatible thread records nested observations at all lengths, and injectivity states that complete prefix data uniquely determines its stream. In practical reconstruction, ambiguity arises from finite depth, missing data, or noise rather than from the exact coherent representation.

For a strange attractor, one would partition an invariant set, label visited regions, and record itineraries. If the partition is generating, sufficiently long symbolic records distinguish trajectories in the relevant sense. The present theory guarantees finite levels, consistency under coarsening, and separation for unrestricted binary histories. It does not provide a generating partition or a topological identification for a named attractor.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The inverse limit reconciles finite models with infinite information. Each $W_n$ is finite, yet a point of $L$ has a compatible component at every depth. Coordinate $k$ of a stream is recoverable at level $k+1$, so the whole system loses no symbolic coordinate even though each individual level forgets the tail.

The construction has deliberate limitations. It uses a binary alphabet, proves injectivity rather than a full equivalence, introduces no topology on $L$, computes no cohomology, and makes no comparison with a concrete smooth system. For an alphabet of size $q$, the same count becomes $q^n$, but system-specific restrictions require separate admissibility arguments.

Several developments are natural. First, the graph diagram should be bundled categorically, with truncations as graph homomorphisms and inverse-system identities proved explicitly. Second, the map $\Psi$ should be shown inverse to $\Phi$. Giving both sides their natural profinite topologies should then yield a homeomorphism with binary Cantor space and its consequences: compactness, total disconnectedness, perfectness, and cardinality information.

Third, graph cochains on finite approximants and the maps induced by truncation can be assembled into a direct system. Its limit may connect finite graph algebra with \v{C}ech-type invariants. Fourth, the shift should be added and the stream--thread correspondence upgraded to a conjugacy. Subshifts of finite type then provide the bridge to constrained symbolic models.

Finally, a Lorenz-specific theorem requires a precise Lorenz map or template, a justified Markov partition, and a verified transition graph. Only then is it appropriate to compare an associated inverse limit and its \v{C}ech cohomology with an attractor. Analogous obligations apply to H\'enon and R\"ossler dynamics.

\section{Conclusion}

Binary words, de Bruijn overlaps, and final-symbol truncation produce a coherent tower of finite directed graphs. Level $n$ has exactly $2^n$ words, truncation preserves edges, and compatible choices form an inverse limit. Every infinite binary stream maps to its family of prefixes, and equality of such families forces equality at every stream coordinate. The map is therefore injective, and the inverse limit contains a full Cantor family of trajectories.

This does not replace geometric attractor analysis. It isolates an algebraic mechanism by which infinitely detailed symbolic dynamics can be retained in finite combinatorial approximations, providing a starting point for categorical, topological, cohomological, and system-specific developments.

\end{document}

