## 4. What this says—and what remains

The inverse limit contains a distinct point for every binary stream and is therefore infinite. With natural topologies, one expects an exact Cantor-space model, but a homeomorphism is a further theorem. A Lorenz-specific conclusion likewise requires a justified Markov partition and transition graph; the unrestricted binary model is a foundation, not that comparison itself.

For background on the topological language, see [inverse limits](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inverse_limit) and [Cantor space](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cantor_space).

<details><summary>Research roadmap</summary>
Bundle the graph system categorically; construct the reverse thread-to-stream map; establish the profinite homeomorphism; compute induced cohomological maps; add shift conjugacy; and only then undertake a system-specific Lorenz comparison.
</details>
